Publish the personalities of the drivers that are linked in statically

A personality is normally published by OSKext, out of the kext's own info
dictionary.  A static archive has no info dictionary: it is not a bundle, it
is not in the prelink record, and there is no OSKext object for it, so
nothing ever read the IOKitPersonalities in its Info.plist.

The prelink record of this kernel holds thirty-six entries and every one of
them is an Apple kext.  The two drivers on this board that matter are not
among them: the platform expert that reads the device tree, and the storage
driver that talks to the UFS controller.  Both are force-loaded static
archives, so both had no way to be matched, their start() was never called,
and their Info.plist files described wishes nobody heard.

That is why the controller was never matched, however many times the device
tree was fixed: the driver was never offered to the matcher in the first
place.  The node in the tree, the compatible string and the probe score
were all correct, and none of them could matter.

The catalogue is the one OSKext publishes into, and the moment to use it is
here: the matching sweep starts in the call below and reads what is in the
catalogue when it starts.  The three personalities are the ones the two
Info.plist files describe, including the second one for the storage driver
that attaches to the root device -- the driver's own start() does no work
unless its provider is one of those.

The name to match the platform expert on is taken from the root nub
itself.  The nub takes its name from the device tree's root node, and the
kernel replaces that name with the first string of the node's compatible
property.  This board forges the identity of another device, so that first
string is the board name and not the generic "device-tree" the upstream
driver was written against; matching a fixed string would never match
anything.

The lines that say what the tree holds are printed next to the personality
line, because that is where the matching starts and because the output
reaches the screen there.  Earlier in the boot there is nowhere to print:
the console is not up yet, and what the kernel writes to the ramoops ring
does not reach the panel.  The registry is used rather than
SecureDTLookupEntry(), because the device tree reader's types live in a
header that only the platform expert sees, and this file is not that file.

---

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOStartIOKit.cpp
@@ -187,6 +187,132 @@
 	 * the way out: a panel that shows match go and no match ret is still
 	 * matching, one that shows both has moved on to bsd_init. */
 	printf("\nnio: IOKit match go");
+	/*
+	 * nio: publish the personalities of the drivers that are linked into
+	 * this kernel as static archives.
+	 *
+	 * A personality is normally published by OSKext, out of the kext's own
+	 * info dictionary.  A static archive has no info dictionary: it is not
+	 * a bundle, it is not in the prelink record, and there is no OSKext
+	 * object for it, so nothing ever read the IOKitPersonalities in its
+	 * Info.plist.  The two drivers on this board that matter -- the
+	 * platform expert which reads the device tree, and the storage driver
+	 * which talks to the UFS controller -- were therefore never matched,
+	 * their start() was never called, and their Info.plist files described
+	 * wishes nobody heard.
+	 *
+	 * The catalogue is the one OSKext publishes into, and this is the
+	 * moment to use it: the matching sweep starts in the call below and
+	 * reads what is in the catalogue when it starts.
+	 */
+	{
+		OSArray      *personalities = OSArray::withCapacity(3);
+		OSDictionary *platformExpert = OSDictionary::withCapacity(4);
+		OSDictionary *storage        = OSDictionary::withCapacity(3);
+		OSDictionary *storageRoot    = OSDictionary::withCapacity(3);
+		OSString     *classPlatform  = OSString::withCString("ArmDTPlatform");
+		OSString     *classStorage   = OSString::withCString("NioUfs");
+		OSString     *providerExpert = OSString::withCString("IOPlatformExpertDevice");
+		OSString     *providerDevice = OSString::withCString("IOPlatformDevice");
+		/*
+		 * The root nub takes its name from the device tree's root
+		 * node, and the kernel replaces that name with the first
+		 * string of the node's compatible property.  This board
+		 * forges the identity of another device, so that first string
+		 * is the board name and not the generic "device-tree" the
+		 * upstream driver was written against.  Taking the name from
+		 * the nub itself matches whatever the tree says, and the line
+		 * below says out loud what it was.
+		 */
+		const char   *rootName      = gRootNub->getName();
+		OSString     *nameMatch      = OSString::withCString(rootName ? rootName : "device-tree");
+		OSNumber     *scorePlatform  = OSNumber::withNumber(5000, 32);
+		OSNumber     *scoreStorage   = OSNumber::withNumber(1000, 32);
+		OSNumber     *scoreRoot      = OSNumber::withNumber(500, 32);
+
+		if (personalities && platformExpert && storage && storageRoot &&
+		    classPlatform && classStorage && providerExpert && providerDevice &&
+		    nameMatch && scorePlatform && scoreStorage && scoreRoot) {
+			platformExpert->setObject("IOClass", classPlatform);
+			platformExpert->setObject("IOProviderClass", providerExpert);
+			platformExpert->setObject("IONameMatch", nameMatch);
+			platformExpert->setObject("IOProbeScore", scorePlatform);
+			personalities->setObject(platformExpert);
+
+			storage->setObject("IOClass", classStorage);
+			storage->setObject("IOProviderClass", providerDevice);
+			storage->setObject("IOProbeScore", scoreStorage);
+			personalities->setObject(storage);
+
+			storageRoot->setObject("IOClass", classStorage);
+			storageRoot->setObject("IOProviderClass", providerExpert);
+			storageRoot->setObject("IOProbeScore", scoreRoot);
+			personalities->setObject(storageRoot);
+
+			printf("\nnio: publishing %d static personalities",
+			       (int) personalities->getCount());
+			gIOCatalogue->addDrivers(personalities, false);
+		}
+
+		/*
+		 * What the tree actually holds, now that it is being read
+		 * for the first time.
+		 *
+		 * The root nub takes its name from the tree, so its name
+		 * says whether the tree was found at all -- if it has no
+		 * name, nothing that matches on one is ever offered the nub,
+		 * the platform expert that reads the tree never starts, the
+		 * nodes under /arm-io are never published, and the storage
+		 * controller is never matched.  That is the whole chain,
+		 * and the name is where it can be seen from.
+		 *
+		 * The nub's children are the tree's children, so the two
+		 * lookups below say whether the branch the storage driver
+		 * needs is in the tree and whether the interrupt controller
+		 * is.
+		 *
+		 * The registry is used rather than SecureDTLookupEntry(),
+		 * because the device tree reader's types live in a header
+		 * that only the platform expert sees, and this file is not
+		 * that file.
+		 *
+		 * These lines land next to the personality line because that
+		 * is where the matching starts and because the output
+		 * reaches the screen there.  Earlier in the boot there is
+		 * nowhere to print: the console is not up yet, and what the
+		 * kernel writes to the ramoops ring does not reach the
+		 * panel.
+		 */
+		{
+			int hasArmIo = 0;
+			int hasIntc  = 0;
+			int hasUfs   = 0;
+			OSIterator *children = gRootNub->getChildIterator(gIODTPlane);
+			OSObject *object;
+			while (children != NULL && (object = children->getNextObject()) != NULL) {
+				IORegistryEntry *node = OSDynamicCast(IORegistryEntry, object);
+				const char *name = node ? node->getName() : NULL;
+				if (name == NULL || strcmp(name, "arm-io") != 0) {
+					continue;
+				}
+				hasArmIo = 1;
+				OSIterator *under = node->getChildIterator(gIODTPlane);
+				OSObject *inner;
+				while (under != NULL && (inner = under->getNextObject()) != NULL) {
+					IORegistryEntry *leaf = OSDynamicCast(IORegistryEntry, inner);
+					const char *leafName = leaf ? leaf->getName() : NULL;
+					if (leafName == NULL) {
+						continue;
+					}
+					if (strcmp(leafName, "intc") == 0) { hasIntc = 1; }
+					if (strcmp(leafName, "ufs")  == 0) { hasUfs  = 1; }
+				}
+			}
+			printf("\nnio: root nub is named %s", rootName ? rootName : "(none)");
+			printf("\nnio: tree has /arm-io=%d /arm-io/intc=%d /arm-io/ufs=%d",
+			       hasArmIo, hasIntc, hasUfs);
+		}
+	}
 	bool ok = gRootNub->startIOServiceMatching();
 	printf("\nnio: IOKit match ret=%d", (int) ok);
 	if (__improbable(!ok)) {
